The plain-text alternative of the sender-dark fixture. The HTML part carries
its own prefers-color-scheme rules and says in words which half is showing.

--alt-0015
Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

<html>
  <head>
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
    <meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light dark">
    <style>
      .shell { background: #fff8e1; color: #3e2723; }
      .when-dark { display: none; }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        .shell { background: #2b2116 !important; color: #ffe0b2 !important; }
        .when-light { display: none !important; }
        .when-dark { display: block !important; }
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body style="margin:0;padding:0;">
    <div class="shell" style="padding:24px;font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;">
      <p class="when-light" style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:20px;">This is the sender's light design.</p>
      <p class="when-dark" style="margin:0 0 12px;font-size:20px;">This is the sender's own dark design.</p>
      <p style="margin:0;">The sentence above names which of the sender's two designs is applied.
        A light page shows the light one and a dark page the dark one, whatever the system is set
        to.</p>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>

--alt-0015--
